The HTML builder emits one route for each `@entry.moth` directory. The directory position determines the output path. No homepage is required.

### Directory-based routes

[codeblock, $code "text":
    @entry.moth              -> index.html
    docs/@entry.moth         -> docs/index.html
    docs/basics/@entry.moth  -> docs/basics/index.html
]

The root module maps to the project index. A nested directory maps to a nested index route. Only the directory position matters. The root filename and ordinary filenames such as `index.moth` have no route significance.

`@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` directories emit no route of their own. They publish APIs only. An ordinary file cannot supply an entry. The builder adds no route override, filename-derived route, page alias or new redirect. Existing `page_url_style` and `redirect_index_html` policies stay unchanged.

### Entry selection

A directory becomes an HTML entry when it contains `@entry.moth`. An empty file, a static-only file and a metadata-only file all count. A direct named builder contribution is a direct const fragment, so this entry still produces a complete document:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

One entry directory produces at most one directory-derived route. `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` never become entries. Imported entries stay dormant.

### Command policy

- Directory-project `check` accepts a valid project with no entries.
- HTML application `build` and `dev` require at least one `@entry.moth` anywhere below the selected project root, not necessarily a homepage.
- Single-file HTML output classifies the explicitly selected root as described below.

The builder does not invent a homepage to satisfy development tooling. Development launch uses the real homepage when one exists, otherwise the lexicographically first normalised entry route. That choice creates no redirect or route alias. Requests to an absent site root retain ordinary missing-route behaviour.

### Single-file entries

A command that explicitly selects one ordinary `.moth` file or one `@entry.moth` compiles it as a synthetic entry:

[codeblock, $code "text":
    moth build page.moth        -> page.html
    moth build @entry.moth      -> index.html
    moth build index.moth       -> index.html
]

A selected `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` keeps its facade role. `check` validates it as an API facade, while an application build reports the no-entry outcome instead of manufacturing a page. Other special root filenames are rejected during source discovery.

The synthetic unit uses the same source identity, module service and error-reporting rules as a directory project. It never creates a filesystem-backed module API.

### Builder ownership

The HTML builder owns route and artefact policy. The frontend remains builder-neutral. The builder decides:

- which `@entry` directories produce HTML artefacts
- how routes are derived
- how output files are organised
- whether to reject unsupported reachable features

Source stays target agnostic. The HTML builder may choose JavaScript, Wasm or mixed output, but source cannot query or branch on that choice. Builder packages expose stable capabilities rather than target identity.

### Output folders

The builder generates output folders and manages them through manifests. Builder settings in `config.moth` own output paths. HTML defaults to `_dev` for development and `_release` for release builds. Every directory output root must be hidden from source discovery. Treat generated folders as build artefacts, not source.

### Release versus development output

The default build writes to the hidden dev folder. The `--release` flag writes to the hidden release output folder. The dev server serves from the dev folder.

### HTML-Wasm output

With `--html-wasm`, the builder can emit a route folder containing `index.html`, `page.js` and `page.wasm` instead of a single HTML file with embedded JavaScript.

### Concepts related to HTML routing and artefacts

- For module roles and root filenames see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For entry runtime, fragments and dependency non-activation see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Entry runtime and fragments).
- For builder slots and entry metadata see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
